A||||7||Spektrum-87GT||do it yourself paint jobs||||1||
Z||000000||Spektrum-87GT||09-22-2001||01:11 AM||||post any and all tips! i'm considering doing it myself, i have access to a very good air compressor... and saved up some money to do it myself. I'm wondering... where would be a good place to get a high quality gun, there is one at walmart for 70 bucks.. and supposedly its professional quality. question #2 is... where the heck do you get paint for it!? i'm really interested in the dupont chroma series paint but they seem like they deal with buisnesses only. And the final question, i'm planning on doing a panel off paint job... what are the parts i need to replace after taking(breaking) them off? and does anyone have a suggestion on where to get them? sorry for the long post, i just want this to turn out good!||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||09-22-2001||01:41 AM||||I paint all my Fieros myself. But I have the space, equipment, and know how. If you've never done it before, you really may want to study up on it....its not like painting w/Krylon. <BR>DuPont Chroma series is what I use as well. You need to decide if you want to use a single stage paint (paint clear coat combo) or a two stage paint (paint then clear). Most solids you can use a single, while metallics you need a two stage. <BR>Make sure you have a decent compressor that can "keep up" with you, and also a decent spray gun. The ones at Wally World are Cambell Housefield I believe, and they are pretty good guns.<BR>Rule of thumb, PREP WORK IS EVERYTHING!!! Remember while sanding and prepping, IF YOU CAN FEEL IT, YOU'LL SEE IT!<BR>Good luck in your painting!<P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B><I>My 7 Fieros</FONT></FONT></B></I>||||reg||1
Z||000002||GTMike||09-22-2001||02:06 AM||||i have painted several cars in my day.  you need to do some reading on it. but you can get a decent paint job doing it your self. <P>i would use a single stage paint. its simpler.  and if you scratch it you can touch it up with out haveing to do the hole pannel.<P>basically you just sand sand sand then prime. then sand again. then probably some more prime then sand.  then wash the car let it dry. the put a light coat on then a medium then the hammer coat.  which is a thick coat.  the trick is putting it on as thick as you can with out getting runs. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||09-22-2001||06:55 AM||||Ditto for whats been said.  You can do a decent paint job yourself.  Metallics shouldn't be your first, it takes practice to lay that stuff down.<P>Spend a lot of time masking, remove molding if you can.  Don't go cheap on materials.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mbramble||09-22-2001||07:54 AM||||As far as replacing moulding fasteners you can get then from the Fiero Store, or from here - <A HREF="http://www.ardeldistributors.com/category.html?UCIDs=414669%7C656537." TARGET=_blank>http://www.ardeldistributors.com/category.html?UCIDs=414669%7C656537.</A>   Rodney Dickman also has a small selection of fasteners.||||reg||1
Z||000005||GodSend||09-22-2001||09:23 AM||||and dont forget to wetsand as well. The nice thing with a base coat clear coat is you can hit it with several layers of each, with some nice wet sanding in between.  ||||reg||1
